Day 9 — Salesforce Flow Automation
📌 Overview
On Day 9 of Salesforce training, I learned and implemented Salesforce Flow Builder to automate business processes in the Gym Management System.

I worked with different types of Flows including:

Screen Flow
Record-Triggered Flow
Scheduled-Triggered Flow
Autolaunched Flow
I also practiced Flow elements such as:

Get Records
Create Records
Update Records
Delete Records
Assignment
Formula Resources
Variables
Choice Resources
Screen Components
Debugging and Activation
🎯 Learning Objectives
Understand Salesforce Flow Builder.
Understand different types of Flows.
Create user-interactive Screen Flows.
Automate processes using Record-Triggered Flows.
Run automation on a daily schedule using Scheduled Flows.
Retrieve Salesforce records using Get Records.
Create and update related records.
Use Flow variables and formulas.
Use Assignments to modify record values.
Debug and activate Flows.
🔹 1. Screen Flow
Definition
A Screen Flow is a Flow that provides a user interface and allows users to enter information and interact with the business process.

Gym Management Implementation
I created Screen Flows for:

Gym-Renew-Membership
Used to renew a member's membership.

Process:

Member Record → Renew Membership Button → Select New Membership Plan → Select Start Date → Calculate Membership Details → Update Member Record → Update Related Membership Record → Display Success Message

Gym-ShowMembers
Used to display member information through a user-facing Flow.

Gym-PaymentDetails
Used to collect and process payment-related information.

🔹 2. Record-Triggered Flow
Definition
A Record-Triggered Flow automatically runs when a Salesforce record is created, updated, or deleted.

Gym Management Implementation
Example:

When a new Member is created:

Member Created → Flow Trigger → Perform Required Automation → Update/Create Related Records

This type of automation is useful when the process should happen automatically without requiring the user to click a button.

🔹 3. Scheduled-Triggered Flow
Definition
A Scheduled-Triggered Flow runs automatically at a specified date, time, or frequency.

Gym Management Implementation
I implemented scheduled automation for membership expiry.

Example:

Every Day → Find Members whose End Date has expired → Process Expired Members → Update/Delete records as required

This demonstrates how Salesforce can perform background automation without requiring a user to manually start the process.

🔹 4. Autolaunched Flow
Definition
An Autolaunched Flow runs in the background without displaying screens to the user.

Gym Management Implementation
I created Autolaunched Flows for background processes such as:

Gym-Delete Record
Gym-Expiry Alert
Gym-Owner Alert
Gym-Welcome Mail
These Flows can be called by other automation or actions.

🔹 5. Flow Elements Practiced
Get Records
Used to retrieve Salesforce records.

Example:

Find a Member based on:

Member ID
End Date
Membership Type
Create Records
Used to create new Salesforce records.

Example:

Create a Membership record after a new Member is registered.

Update Records
Used to modify existing Salesforce records.

Example:

Update:

Membership Type
Join Date
End Date
Membership Fee
Status
Delete Records
Used to remove Salesforce records.

Example:

Process expired Member records based on business requirements.

Assignment
Used to assign values to variables or record fields.

Example:

Member Membership Type
Selected New Plan

Member Join Date
Selected Start Date

🔹 6. Flow Variables
I practiced creating variables to store values during Flow execution.

Examples:

MemberId
CurrentPlan
NewPlan
StartDate
EndDate
NewFee
NewDiscount
Variables allow information to be passed between different Flow elements.

🔹 7. Formula Resources
I used formulas to calculate values dynamically.

Examples:

Membership Duration
End Date - Start Date

Membership Fee
Monthly → ₹1000 Quarterly → ₹2900 Half Yearly → ₹5500 Yearly → ₹11000

Discount
Monthly → 10% Quarterly → 15% Half Yearly → 20% Yearly → 25%

Current Date
TODAY()

These formulas were used to implement business logic inside the Gym Management System.

🔹 8. Flow Logic
I practiced controlling Flow execution using:

Conditions
Decisions
Assignments
Formulas
Variables
Example:

If Membership Type = Monthly → Fee = ₹1000 → Discount = 10%

If Membership Type = Quarterly → Fee = ₹2900 → Discount = 15%

If Membership Type = Half Yearly → Fee = ₹5500 → Discount = 20%

If Membership Type = Yearly → Fee = ₹11000 → Discount = 25%

🔹 9. Flow Debugging
I practiced debugging Flows before activation.

Debugging helped me verify:

Input values
Record values
Variable values
Formula results
Record updates
Flow execution path
After successful testing, the Flows were activated.

🏋️ Gym Management System Automation
The following Flows were implemented during this phase:

Flow	Type	Purpose
Gym-Delete Record	Autolaunched	Handles record deletion
Gym-Expiry Alert	Autolaunched	Handles membership expiry notification
Gym-Owner Alert	Autolaunched	Notifies gym owner about relevant events
Gym-PaymentDetails	Screen Flow	Collects/processes payment information
Gym-Renew-Membership	Screen Flow	Renews a member's membership
Gym-ShowMembers	Screen Flow	Displays member information
Gym-Welcome Mail	Autolaunched	Sends welcome communication
🔄 Overall Flow Architecture
                    GYM MANAGEMENT SYSTEM
                            |
             +--------------+--------------+
             |              |              |
             ↓              ↓              ↓
        Screen Flow   Record-Triggered   Scheduled
             |              |              |
             ↓              ↓              ↓
        User Input      Record Event    Daily/Time
             |              |              |
             +--------------+--------------+
                            |
                            ↓
                   Flow Business Logic
                            |
             +--------------+--------------+
             |              |              |
             ↓              ↓              ↓
        Get Records   Update Records   Create Records
                            |
                            ↓
                    Salesforce Database
